ate to fever", "History of well-controlled asthma (suggesting no new pulmonary pathology as source of symptoms)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Bacterial Superinfection of Sinusitis", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.552901023890785, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Absence of trauma or epistaxis history weakens vascular complications as a cause of bloody discharge."], "evidence_against": ["No single finding decisively contradicts bacterial superinfection, though the absence of epistaxis history slightly reduces its likelihood."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Orbital Infection Secondary to Sinusitis", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.25, "posterior": 0.30716723549488056, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["The age profile moderately supports orbital infection as a potential complication, though the lack of localized orbital signs weakens this."], "evidence_against": ["No single finding decisively contradicts bacterial superinfection, though the absence of epistaxis history slightly reduces its likelihood."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Intracranial Complication of Sinusitis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.051, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["No single finding decisively contradicts bacterial superinfection, though the absence of epistaxis history slightly reduces its likelihood."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Vascular Complication of Sinusitis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.034, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of trauma or epistaxis history weakens vascular complications as a cause of bloody discharge."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Foreign Body Obstruction with Secondary Infection", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.041, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B6": {"id": "B6", "label": "Neoplastic Process in the Nasal Region", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.014, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Streptococcus pneumoniae sinusitis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.18430034129692832, "posterior": 0.18430034129692832, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.25, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Haemophilus influenzae sinusitis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.15358361774744028, "posterior": 0.15358361774744028, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.2, "interrupt_relevance": 0.35, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "MRSA sinusitis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.12286689419795223, "posterior": 0.12286689419795223, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.15, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.4": {"id": "B1.4", "label": "Moraxella catarrhalis sinusitis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.09215017064846416, "posterior": 0.09215017064846416, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.7, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.1, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4", "B2"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute worsening of symptoms following decongestant treatment with purulent nasal discharge mixed with blood and constitutional symptoms (fever, tachycardia) support bacterial superinfecti…", "summary": "The purulent discharge with blood and fever/tachycardia strongly support bacterial superinfection as the most likely progression of acute sinusitis."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 7-year-old age and presence of tachycardia disproportionate to fever support orbital infection as a high-risk complication of sinusitis?", "summary": "The age profile moderately supports orbital infection as a potential complication, though the lack of localized orbital signs weakens this."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the normal cardiopulmonary exam and absence of visual acuity changes sufficient to argue against orbital complications?", "summary": "Normal cardiopulmonary findings and no visual changes provide moderate evidence against orbital infection, which typically presents with more focal neurological signs."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the absence of neurological symptoms (headache, altered mental status) rule out intracranial complications despite high danger score?", "summary": "Lack of neurological symptoms weakens the case for intracranial complications, though the high danger score keeps this a potential consideration."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of epistaxis or nasal trauma history rule out Kiesselbach plexus vascular complications despite the bloody discharge?", "summary": "Absence of trauma or epistaxis history weakens vascular complications as a cause of bloody discharge."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Bacterial Superinfection of Sinusitis? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "No single finding decisively contradicts bacterial superinfection, though the absence of epistaxis history slightly reduces its likelihood."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.553], ["B2", 0.307], ["B1.1", 0.184]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers are present. The patient's vital signs (BP 90/48 mmHg, pulse 124/min) indicate mild tachycardia but no end-organ signs of shock. Oxygen saturation is normal, and there is no airway compromise. Unilateral nasal discharge with blood does not suggest uncontrolled haemorrhage.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading hypothesis B1 (55.3%) lacks sufficient posterior to trigger confirmation threshold. Dangerous alternative B2 (30.7%) remains unresolved with posterior >=0.10 and danger >=0.7. Specific bacterial subtypes (B1.1-B1.4) and orbital infection (B2) require further discriminators to clarify management priorities."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 1.0, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.30716723549488056, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Bleeding and infected vessel of Kiesselbach plexus"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Foreign body obstruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Nasopharyngeal carcinoma"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Septal perforation"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Sinusitis with bacterial superinfection"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Sinus infection', 'Chronic sinusitis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.553}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Orbital Infection Secondary to Sinusitis", "posterior": 0.307, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 2, "disconfirming": 4, "last_disconfirming_digest": "No single finding decisively contradicts bacterial superinfection, though the absence of epistaxis history slightly red…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=43%, source=both]\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae sinusitis vs Haemophilus influenzae sinusitis:\n  Favours first:  chills, malaise, rapid heart rate, sweating\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, altered mental status, anxiety, blisters, decreased range of motion\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae sinusitis vs MRSA sinusitis:\n  Favours first:  chest pain, chills, confusion, difficulty breathing, malaise\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n\nHaemophilus influenzae sinusitis vs MRSA sinusitis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, altered mental status, blisters, chest pain, confusion\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n\nRelated to MRSA sinusitis: chronic rhinosinusitis, ethmoid sinusitis, frontal sinusitis\n\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.60):]\n  Source: Periorbital and orbital cellulitis. > Epidemiology\n  Overall, orbital cellulitis is much more common in younger children than in older children or adults. This is because most cases of orbital cellulitis are preceded by bacterial rhinosinusitis or an up\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.66):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  In contrast, chronic invasive sinusitis is a slowly destructive process that most commonly affects the ethmoid and sphenoid sinuses but can involve any sinus. Patients are usually but not always immun\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.51):]\n  Source: Orbital cellulitis. > Pathophysiology\n  Orbital cellulitis is a rare complication of bacterial rhinosinusitis. However, in most cases of orbital cellulitis, rhinosinusitis is the source of infection. Data show that in up to 86% to 98% of ca\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.56):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  In contrast, chronic invasive sinusitis is a slowly destructive process that most commonly affects the ethmoid and sphenoid sinuses but can involve any sinus. Patients are usually but not always immun\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  worsening symptoms after initial decongestant treatment → bacterial etiology with resistance to initial therapy → MRSA sinusitis\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate nasal discharge for MRSA colonization through culture testing, given the failure of decongestant therapy and persistent symptoms\n  nasal discharge with worsening pain → progression to orbital cellulitis → Orbital Infection Secondary to Sinusitis\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Assess for periorbital swelling or proptosis that might indicate orbital cellulitis as a complication of sinusitis\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
